npu_monitor_poll_rate_npu.log -- the NPU monitor's polling rate: what Windows' GPU-engine
counter reads for the NPU at 0.1 / 0.25 / 0.5 s, how exact the poll period is, and whether a
10 Hz monitor disturbs a running kernel.

Desktop 2: Ryzen 7 8700G, Phoenix XDNA1 (NPU driver 32.0.20101.3760, firmware 1.5.5.391,
XRT 2.21.0), Windows 11. tools/hwinfo_npu_bridge.cpp at the two-cadence build committed in
e776647 (--interval for the engine counters + XRT clock, --smi-interval for xrt-smi on its
own thread), then with the timing change this log accompanies (timeBeginPeriod(1), sleep to
the deadline, fixed-rate schedule). 2026-09-07 23:43 to 2026-09-08. Three sessions were
using this machine that evening; the sections say which runs were contaminated and by what.

WHY
---
The monitor's floor was 0.5 s with xrt-smi (a child process, 130-400 ms per report) inside
the poll loop. With xrt-smi moved to its own cadence the fast sources cost ~1 ms (PDH) and
~0.4 ms (XRT clock query) per poll, so the floor can drop to 0.1 s -- but a faster PDH sample
is only useful if the engine counter still reads something meaningful at that window, and
the loop must actually keep the period it claims. Both were measured rather than assumed.

WHAT WAS RUN
------------
test_rate_load.sh: three monitors side by side (--plain --no-hwinfo --interval 0.1 / 0.25 /
0.5), started 2 s before a 2048^3 bf16 64/64/64 --c-single-buffer 1 GEMM hold (hold_npu.sh:
whole_array.py --iters 5000, ~39 s), killed 2 s after it. Analysis: per-file period stats,
the "busy stretch" = first to last poll over 5 %, its mean/min/max, and polls under 5 %
inside it (dropouts). The user's own bridge instance (default arguments, 0.5 s, publishing
to HWiNFO) was running throughout every run below.

--- test_rate_load.sh ---
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Three monitors at 0.1 / 0.25 / 0.5 s, side by side, across one 2048^3 bf16 GEMM hold on the NPU.
# Purpose: see what the Windows engine counter actually reads at each rate under the same load
# (functional check of the monitor, not a benchmark -- an AdaRound job is running on the CPU).
cd /c/Users/<user>/PycharmProjects/ryzen-ai-xdna1-quantization || exit 1
T="$CLAUDE_JOB_DIR/tmp"
EXE=./tools/hwinfo_npu_bridge.exe
for iv in 0.1 0.25 0.5; do
  $EXE --plain --no-hwinfo --interval $iv > "$T/rate_load_$iv.txt" 2>&1 &
done
sleep 2
echo "hold start $(date +%H:%M:%S)"
bash "$T/hold_npu.sh" > "$T/hold_npu6.txt" 2>&1
echo "hold end   $(date +%H:%M:%S)"
sleep 2
for p in $(jobs -p); do kill $p 2>/dev/null; done
wait 2>/dev/null
cat "$T/hold_npu6.txt"
python - "$T" <<'EOF'
import re, sys, os
T = sys.argv[1]
for iv in ("0.1", "0.25", "0.5"):
    vals, per = [], []
    for l in open(os.path.join(T, "rate_load_%s.txt" % iv), encoding="utf-8"):
        m = re.search(r"util=([\d.]+|n/a)%.*period=([\d.]+|n/a)s", l)
        if not m: continue
        if m.group(1) != "n/a": vals.append(float(m.group(1)))
        if m.group(2) != "n/a": per.append(float(m.group(2)))
    busy = [v for v in vals if v > 5]
    zeros_in_busy = 0
    # inside the busy stretch, count polls that read 0 (a value the driver did not update in time)
    if busy:
        first = next(i for i, v in enumerate(vals) if v > 5); last = len(vals) - 1 - next(i for i, v in enumerate(reversed(vals)) if v > 5)
        stretch = vals[first:last + 1]
        zeros_in_busy = sum(1 for v in stretch if v < 5)
        print("interval %s: %d polls, period mean %.3f s (min %.3f max %.3f); busy stretch %d polls: mean %.1f%% min %.1f%% max %.1f%%, %d polls under 5%% inside it" % (
            iv, len(vals), sum(per)/len(per), min(per), max(per), len(stretch), sum(stretch)/len(stretch), min(stretch), max(stretch), zeros_in_busy))
        print("   first 30 busy readings:", " ".join("%.0f" % v for v in stretch[:30]))
    else:
        print("interval %s: %d polls, no busy readings" % (iv, len(vals)))
EOF

--- hold_npu.sh ---
export VIRTUAL_ENV='C:\Users\<user>\mlir-aie\ironenv'
export PATH="C:\Users\<user>\mlir-aie\ironenv\Scripts:C:\Users\<user>\mlir-aie\ironenv\Lib\site-packages\mlir_aie\bin:C:\Users\<user>\mlir-aie\ironenv\Lib\site-packages\mlir_aie\lib:C:\Xilinx\XRT\xrt_sdk\xrt\lib:C:\Xilinx\XRT\xrt_sdk\xrt:C:\Windows\System32\AMD:$PATH"
export MLIR_AIE_INSTALL_DIR='C:\Users\<user>\mlir-aie\ironenv\Lib\site-packages\mlir_aie'
export PEANO_INSTALL_DIR='C:\Users\<user>\mlir-aie\ironenv\Lib\site-packages\llvm-aie'
export XRT_ROOT='C:\Xilinx\XRT\xrt_sdk\xrt'
export PYTHONPATH='C:\Xilinx\XRT\xrt_sdk\xrt\python'
export NPU2='0'; unset XILINX_XRT
cd ~/mlir-aie/programming_examples/basic/matrix_multiplication/whole_array || exit 1
python whole_array.py --dev npu -M 2048 -K 2048 -N 2048 -m 64 -k 64 -n 64 --dtype_in bf16 --dtype_out f32 --c-single-buffer 1 --warmup 3 --iters 5000 2>&1 | tail -4


